//  PRIMARY HAND ONLY. Alt-hand casting is deliberately removed for now: every
//  generated staff rolls a random altAbility, and an ability with no npcIntent
//  patch falls back to "harmful", so the alt branch aimed at the combat target
//  and overwrote the primary branch's aim in the same tick. Aim position is a
//  single shared resource -- whichever branch writes it last wins. Re-add alt
//  only under the same one-cast-per-tick selector.
//
//  MOVEMENT comes from the combat coordinator stagehand, same as vanilla melee.
//  The coordinator hands each member a slot position around the target and
//  nicemice_wandstaff-approach paths to it with moveToPosition, which both
//  closes and retreats -- if the assigned slot is further out than we are
//  standing, pathing to it walks us backwards.
//
//  The group and task joins MUST run every tick: BGroup:updateGroups() drops
//  any group or task that was not re-joined during the current update, and
//  without a joined task the coordinator allocates no slot and every
//  groupResource lookup comes back empty. That is why the previous copy of the
//  approach behavior did nothing -- nothing in this tree had ever joined.
//
//  Casting is listed before movement because setAimPosition sets
//  self.setFacingDirection, and moveToPosition only takes over facing when that
//  flag is clear -- so an NPC that is aiming walks its slot while still facing
//  its target.
//
//  Casting order is intent priority: heal, then buff, then attack. Only one
//  leaf may own the aim on any given tick.
//
//  castRangeFallback / castRangeMargin: cast range is read off the equipped
//  ability (vanilla staff abilities declare maxCastRange, all of them 25 tiles).
//  The fallback only applies when an ability declares none; the margin covers
//  the gap between the ability's "focus position" and our entity position.
//
//  requireLineOfSight: heal/buff ally scans reject targets we cannot see. Turn
//  off in walled test arenas where allies sit behind pillars.
//
//  debugMovement also logs the slot position and the min/max range the
//  coordinator actually hands back, which is the number we need in order to
//  tune standoff.
